Some members of the public worried that the Large Hadron Collider might create a black hole that could destroy the Earth. Physicists respond that if microscopic black holes could be produced at all, they would evaporate almost instantly via Hawking radiation, so they could not grow. Independently, they point out that cosmic rays with far higher energies than the LHC have been striking the Earth, the Moon, and other dense bodies for billions of years without creating dangerous black holes, which they argue shows the process is safe. Because these astronomical bodies still exist, the observed survival of the Moon and Sun under cosmic-ray bombardment supports the conclusion that collider energies pose no such risk. Taken together, the evaporation argument and the cosmic-ray argument are offered as mutually reinforcing reasons to reject the doomsday scenario.

